= Performance of Monte Carlo integration in different dimensions

#task[
  We would like to compare the performance of the Monte Carlo integration technique with the regular midpoint method. To this end, consider the integral
  $ I = integral_V f(bx) dif^d bx, $
  where the integration domain $V$ is a $d$-dimensional hypercube with $0 <= x_i <= 1$ for each component of the vector $bx = (x_1, x_2, ..., x_d)$. The function we want to integrate is given by
  $ f(bx) = product_(i=1)^d 3/2 (1 - x_i^2). $
  This has an analytic solution of course, which is $I = 1$ independent of $d$, but we want to ignore this for the moment and use the problem as a test of the relative performance of Monte Carlo integration and ordinary integration techniques. To this end, calculate the integral for $d in {1, 2, 3, ..., 10}$, using
  + the midpoint method, where you divide the volume into a set of much smaller hypercubes obtained by subdividing each axis into $n$ intervals, and where you approximate the integral by evaluating the function at the centers of the small cubes.
  + standard Monte Carlo integration in $d$ dimensions, using $N$ random vectors.
  For definiteness, adopt $n = 6$ and $N = 20 thin 000$. For both of the methods, report the numerical result for $I$ and the CPU-time needed for each of the dimensions. (If you manage, you can also go to slightly higher dimension.)
]

